## Why This Is Asked
Tests bridging a C object with Go, memory ownership, and safe cross-language concurrency using CGO. It also touches resource lifecycle (Close) and error handling when C returns NULL.

## Key Concepts
- CGO bindings for C structs and lifecycle
- Memory ownership and Free/Close semantics
- Thread-safety in C with a mutex vs. Go-level synchronization
- Cross-language error handling and result propagation

## Code Example
```c
// counter.h
typedef struct AtomicCounter AtomicCounter;
AtomicCounter* ac_new(void);
void ac_inc(AtomicCounter* ac);
int ac_get(AtomicCounter* ac);
void ac_free(AtomicCounter* ac);
```
```c
// counter.c (stub with mutex)
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
typedef struct AtomicCounter {
  int val;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
} AtomicCounter;
AtomicCounter* ac_new(void){ AtomicCounter* a = malloc(sizeof(AtomicCounter)); a->val=0; pthread_mutex_init(&a->m, NULL); return a; }
void ac_inc(AtomicCounter* a){ pthread_mutex_lock(&a->m); a->val++; pthread_mutex_unlock(&a->m); }
int ac_get(AtomicCounter* a){ pthread_mutex_lock(&a->m); int v=a->val; pthread_mutex_unlock(&a->m); return v; }
void ac_free(AtomicCounter* a){ pthread_mutex_destroy(&a->m); free(a); }
```
```go
// counter.go
package counter
/*
#include "counter.h"
#include <stdlib.h>
*/
import "C"
import (
  "errors"
)

type AtomicCounter struct{ c *C.AtomicCounter }

func NewAtomicCounter() (*AtomicCounter, error) {
  p := C.ac_new()
  if p == nil {
    return nil, errors.New("failed to create AtomicCounter")
  }
  return &AtomicCounter{c: p}, nil
}

func (a *AtomicCounter) Inc() { C.ac_inc(a.c) }
func (a *AtomicCounter) Get() int { return int(C.ac_get(a.c)) }
func (a *AtomicCounter) Close() { C.ac_free(a.c) }
```

```go
// main.go
package main
import (
  "fmt"
  "sync"
  "counter" // path to the package
)

func main() {
  ac, _ := counter.NewAtomicCounter()
  var wg sync.WaitGroup
  increments := 1000
  for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
    wg.Add(1)
    go func() {
      for j := 0; j < increments; j++ { ac.Inc() }
      wg.Done()
    }()
  }
  wg.Wait()
  fmt.Println("final:", ac.Get())
  ac.Close()
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support multiple independent counters in the same process?
- What changes to CGO flags or build tags would you add to ensure pthreads compatibility on all platforms?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CGOA wiring with callbacks, thread safety, and per-listener lifetimes, not just simple wrappers.

## Key Concepts
- CGO callbacks and //export usage
- Passing a Go-visible context to C without raw Go pointers
- Thread safety and runtime constraints when C calls Go
- Cleanup/unregister patterns and backpressure considerations

## Code Example
```c
// evt.h
typedef void (*event_cb)(int event, const char* message, void* ctx);
void register_event_listener(event_cb cb, void* ctx);
void unregister_event_listener(void* ctx);
```
```c
// evt.c (minimal emitter)
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include "evt.h"

static struct { event_cb cb; void* ctx; } listeners[16];
static int n=0; static pthread_t t;
static int run=1;
void* emitter(void*){ int i=0; while(run){ sleep(1); for(int k=0;k<n;k++) listeners[k].cb(i, "msg", listeners[k].ctx); i++; } return NULL; }
void register_event_listener(event_cb cb, void* ctx){ if(n<16){ listeners[n++]={cb,ctx}; if(n==1) pthread_create(&t, NULL, emitter, NULL); }}
void unregister_event_listener(void* ctx){ for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(listeners[i].ctx==ctx){ for(int j=i;j<n-1;j++) listeners[j]=listeners[j+1]; n--; break;} if(n==0) run=0; }
```
```go
// evt.go
package main
/*
#include "evt.h"
*/
import "C"
import (
  "sync"
  "unsafe"
)

type Event struct{ Event int; Message string }

var listeners sync.Map // ctx -> chan Event

//export goEventCallback
func goEventCallback(event C.int, msg *C.char, ctx unsafe.Pointer) {
  if v,ok := listeners.Load(ctx); ok {
    ch := v.(chan Event)
    ch <- Event{int(event), C.GoString(msg)}
  }
}

func StartListener() chan Event {
  ch := make(chan Event, 8)
  // In a real implementation, generate a unique ctx per listener and pass it to C
  ctx := unsafe.Pointer(uintptr(new(int)))
  listeners.Store(ctx, ch)
  C.register_event_listener((C.event_cb)(C.goEventCallback), ctx)
  return ch
}

func StopListener(ctx unsafe.Pointer) { C.unregister_event_listener(ctx) }
```

